# What an agent may do in a page

An agent talking to the user from a chat of the page — Syrtis, Claude through symfony-ai — acts in the page through tools the page itself offers, and reads the page as a tree of the components it shows. The loader holds the mechanics; the design system writes what each component says of itself. Nothing is listed by hand: a component declares what it is and what may be done to it, and the agent discovers it.

## The tools of the page

`AgentToolsService` registers six tools of its own, and whatever tools the components of the page add while they are mounted (`getAgentTools()` on a render node). An agent's scenario declares only the first two: the rest it finds.

| Tool | What it does |
|---|---|
| `list_tools({scope?, filter?})` | The tools available right now, with their schema. |
| `call_tool({name, arguments})` | Runs one of them. |
| `inspect_page({node?, depth?})` | The page as a tree: `{locale, nodes}`, each node with its id, kind, label, description, state and actions. |
| `act({node, action, arguments})` | Runs an action of a node. |
| `find_nodes({query, type?})` | The nodes whose kind, label or description holds a text, each with the way to it from the top of the page. |
| `focus({nodes})` | Shows the user pieces of the page: unfolds what hides them, scrolls to the first, frames them all. |

Arguments given flat beside the name — `{name: 'act', node, action, value}` — are taken as the tool's, and a piece and an action given with no name at all as `act`'s: agents do it, and nothing is gained by refusing. A call with neither is told what `call_tool` takes, not that a tool named "undefined" is missing.

A call reaches the page as a message of the conversation; `AgentToolsService.run(callId, name, args, context)` runs it once whatever publishes it twice, and the chat sends the answer back. `context.caller` names the conversation, so that what a tool leaves in the page — a frame — is that agent's beside another's.

## A node

A node is an instance of a component that has a contract: a `.ai.yml` beside its template, read by `AgentContractService`.

{% raw %}```yaml
# components/switch/switch.ai.yml
type: switch
description: A switch, on or off.
actions:
  get:
    description: Tells whether the switch is on.
    annotations: { readOnlyHint: true }
  set:
    description: Turns the switch on or off.
    input_schema:
      properties: { checked: { type: boolean } }
      required: [checked]
  toggle:
    description: Turns the switch to the other state, as a click does.
```{% endraw %}

- Written in english and never translated: a description is an instruction, as a schema is, and one wording is what gets tested. What the agent finds in the user's language is what the user sees — the labels.
- `extends: '@Bundle/components/switch/switch'` makes a contract another's, with its own `description`: `color-scheme-switch` is a switch, and only says what it is for.
- One contract serves every twin of the component's directory — `switch.ts` and `switch.vue` alike — and each has to run every action it declares; one that does not is left out of the page, and said in the console.

The script runs the actions and says the rest, through the same hooks whatever draws the component:

| Hook | Render node (`.ts`) | Vue (`.vue`, as methods) |
|---|---|---|
| The actions, by name | `getAgentActions(): AgentActionsImplementation` | `getAgentActions()` |
| The state, read at each inspection | `getAgentState()` | `getAgentState()` |
| The label, as the user reads it | `getAgentLabel()` | `getAgentLabel()` |
| The element a frame goes round | `getAgentFrameElement()` | `getAgentFrameElement()` |
| Making a node it holds visible before an agent points at it | `revealAgentChild(child)` | `revealAgentChild(child)` |

Both kinds implement `AgentNodeProviderInterface` — a vue through `VueAgentNode` — and register with the service as they mount, a render node in `activateTools()`, a vue from the global mixin. The contracts of the vues drawn in a page travel once per view (`vueContracts` of the layout's render data); a vue finds its own by its `viewPath`, or by the id of its template when it was imported rather than registered by name.

## What the page is to the agent

`AgentPage` is the page taken at one moment: every registered node standing in the document, nested as its element is — not as any render tree holds it, where an item of the side menu is the layout's and not the menu's. A node not shown is skipped and what it holds raised to its place.

A node is shown when it says so, or, saying nothing, when what holds it is:

- `{ agent: true }` where a component is drawn, or the actions it offers, `{ agent: ['get', 'toggle'] }`; `{ agent: false }` holds it back — the `button-target`s inside a table's cells say so, reached through the table instead;
- `data-agent` on an element exposes everything under it: `layout_agent = true` in a layout sets it on the body, and the whole application is read;
- `data-agent="false"` holds a region back.

## Parts of a node

A row, a cell, an item of a menu are not nodes: hundreds of them would drown the agent. They are reached through an action of the node holding them — `point_row({row})` on a table, `point({item})` on a menu —, which frames them for the agent that asked:

{% raw %}```ts
this.app.services.agentTools?.pointer.point([element], context);
```{% endraw %}

`AgentPointer` keeps what each caller points at: a pointing replaces the caller's last, and a frame stays until the user acts in its layer of the page or the agent points elsewhere. The frames themselves are drawn by `AgentFrameLayer`, in a layer over the page: an outline worn by an element is cut by the first ancestor hiding its overflow, one drawn there never is. One layer per layer of the page — the page's own under the veil of an overlay, one in each modal or panel (`OverlayService.layerOf`) —, so a row pointed at waits dimmed under a panel opened over it, and is there again once the panel closes; the user acting in the panel takes back the panel's frames, not the page's.

## Leaving the page

An action that leaves the page — a link followed, a form sent — returns an `AgentNavigation` (an address, or a press that will leave) rather than acting at once. The chat holds the answer in the tab for its conversation (`AgentHeldResults`, in `sessionStorage`, two minutes), lets the page go, and the chat of the page reached sends it, with where it arrived (`arrived`) and what the page said in toasts (`notifications`). A press that ends up not leaving — a form sent in the background — is answered from where it was made after two seconds.

## What the user did meanwhile

Between two of its turns the agent is blind: the user opens a panel, searches a table, changes page, and the agent reads the difference without its cause. So the page writes down what the user does (`AgentUserEvents`, in `sessionStorage`, the last ten), and the chat sends it with whatever goes to the agent next — a message, a tool's answer — through `takeUserEvents()`, given to `postMessage(content, userEvents)` and `postToolResult(call, callId, results, userEvents)`. An event is `{at, kind, summary, count, node?, detail?}`, the summary a sentence in english: `Switched "Theme" on`. The same thing done again is counted, not written again: a switch flipped forty times is one line.

- The loader writes the pages reached (`navigate`) and the veils opened or closed (`overlay`) on its own.
- A component says the rest: `this.logAgentEvent('Searched "pfn" in the table "Models"', { key: 'search' })` on a render node or a vue, which dispatches `agent:event` on its element with the node's kind and label; `key` says what counts as the same thing again — the node and the action —, the node and the sentence by default. What matters is that it was done, not what it holds: a field says it was edited, once, when the user is done with it.
- What the agent does itself is not written down: nothing is, while one of its calls runs or just after, and the page its action leaves for is told so before it goes.

The develop toolbar's *User events* tab shows the log as it stands.

## Seeing it

The develop toolbar's *Agent tools* tab shows the page as `inspect_page` returns it, runs each action, and *Show on page* frames every component that has a contract: solid when the agent is shown it, dashed when it could be.
